  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe 500MB ridge over the central CONUS will shift east as \nnorthwest flow sets up over the Great Lakes and into the Upper\nOhio Valley. This will set up a series of shortwaves to travel\nsoutheast over the region the next few days. The timing of the\nshortwaves over the next few days will be the main issue as hi-\nres models and ensembles are not handling the setup very well \nin the timing and placement. \n\nThere is a conditional potential for severe storms today. If\nstorms can develop, the ML CAPE between 2000-3000 J/kg and\n25-30kt 0-6 km shear would support severe storms. However, \nconsensus in the models suggests a more early Wednesday morning\nthreat with the potential development of storms moving in off\nthe Great Lakes.\n\nThere will be a second threat Wednesday during the late \nafternoon and evening. Current indications are the ridge should \nbegin to break down late Wednesday and Thursday, as a shortwave \nand surface cold front approaching. The potential for severe \nstorms will also increase by Wednesday evening, increasing again\non Thursday as convection increases in diurnal heating along \nthe slow moving front. Increasing NW flow aloft should result in\nincreased shear and diurnal instability. The potential \ncontinues for severe storms into Thursday. The latest ML \nguidance indicates the increasing severe potential, especially \nlate Wednesday and Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA 500mb ridge extending over the Great Lakes and Ohio River\nValley will help bring in the warmer temperatures through\nThursday. The heat should peak Wednesday with highs in the \nupper 80s to lower 90s for most areas. This combined with higher \nhumidity is likely to result in heat index values in the upper\n90s to 100 Wednesday. A Heat Advisory was issued for eastern\nOhio, most of northern West Virginia, and southwestern PA for\nareas that are likely to reach heat indices of 100F.\n\nHeat is forecast to linger into Thursday as the 500mb ridge\nweakens and moves southeastward. Temperatures in the upper 80s\nand lower 90s with heat indices in the upper 90s is expected. A\ntrough moving into the area behind the ridge will bring cloud\ncover and the chance for showers/storms which could inhibit\ndaytime heating.",
